diff --git a/drivers/mtd/chips/map_esp32.c b/drivers/mtd/chips/map_esp32.c
--- a/drivers/mtd/chips/map_esp32.c
+++ b/drivers/mtd/chips/map_esp32.c
@@ -1,19 +1,43 @@
 // SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+/*
+ * MTD chip driver for ESP32 chips, read-only.
+ *
+ * The flash is memory-mapped at 0x42000000 and the kernel already executes
+ * from it (CONFIG_XIP_KERNEL), so reading it is a copy out of that mapping
+ * and needs no help from anything else on the chip.
+ *
+ * Writing is a different matter. Only the ROM's own SPI routine can program
+ * this flash, and on this port nothing on the other core serves flash
+ * requests any more: the firmware's IPC, its flash server and its transports
+ * were removed so that core 0 could be handed to Linux. That is the whole
+ * point of the port, and it is why this driver is read-only.
+ *
+ * So the IPC half of this driver is gone: no esp32_ipc_flash_* command
+ * objects, no completion, no lock, no platform driver for the
+ * "esp,esp32-ipc-flash" node. What is left reads through map_copy_from(),
+ * which is physmap's own helper over map->virt.
+ *
+ * The partition table is not read here at all any more. It used to be
+ * fetched through the IPC, and then out of the mapping at 0x8000; both are
+ * gone. The partitions are now declared in the device tree under the flash
+ * node with compatible = "fixed-partitions", which is the generic parser
+ * every memory-mapped-flash board uses. It reads the DT and never touches
+ * the flash, so there is nothing here that can fail on a read, and the
+ * driver has no partition code left in it.
+ *
+ * erase and write return -EROFS. MTD_WRITEABLE is cleared so that the MTD
+ * core refuses them before they are reached, and the callbacks refuse them
+ * as well in case something calls them directly.
+ */
 
